'''argid''' (är′jid), ''n.'' A fish of the family ''Argidæ.''

'''Argidæ''' (är′ji-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Arges'' + ''-idæ.''] A family of nematognathous fishes, typified by the genus ''Arges'', related to the ''Loricariidæ'', but having a naked body and only maxillary barbels. {{smaller|There are about 10 known species, of small size, inhabiting the upper Andean streams and derivatives therefrom.}}

'''argil''' (är′jil), ''n.'' [< F. ''argile'', < L. ''argilla'', white clay, < Gr. ἄργιλλα or ἄργιλα, usually ἄργιλλος or ἄργιλος, white clay, < ἀργός, white: see ''argent.''] Potters’ clay. {{smaller|This word has been used in different senses, and was proposed as a name for alumina when its nature was first discovered. It is now used by technical writers as a distinctive term for clay which is fit for potters’ use.}}

'''argillaceous''' (är-ji-lā′shius), ''a.'' [< L. ''argillaceus'', < ''argilla'', white clay: see ''argil.''] '''1.''' Of the nature of or resembling clay.—'''2.''' Containing a considerable amount of clayey matter: as, ''argillaceous'' earth.—'''Argillaceous rocks,''' {{smaller|rocks of sedimentary origin, soft in texture, deposited for the most part in thin layers. Clay forms the basis, but with it other substances may be associated, as vegetable matter (carbonaceous shale), iron (clayband ironstone), lime (marl), etc. When the shale is tolerably pure it is readily distinguished by the peculiar odor, termed ''argillaceous'', which it emits when breathed on.}}—'''Argillaceous slate''' or '''schist,''' {{smaller|clay slate, a metamorphic rock which in Scotland is characteristic of the Silurian formation.}}

